 "transcript": {
  "answer": "You're not imagining it, but it's mostly not because the price of power jumped. In Perth, the electricity tariff has barely moved. What changed is that the government bill credits that used to shrink people's bills have stopped.\n\n**The tariff itself has risen only a little**\n- Perth electricity prices were up **2.4% in the year to August 2026**, which is the latest ABS monthly CPI. All of that rise came from the July 2026 price change. The month before that, July 2025, prices rose 2.5%.\n- Across all capital cities, electricity was up **13.2%** over the same year. Perth has had a much smaller price rise than the rest of the country.\n\n**What you actually pay has risen a lot, because the credits ended**\nThe ABS counts government energy credits as a price cut, so the Perth electricity index shows when they were applied:\n\n| Period | Perth electricity index |\n|---|---|\n| Aug 2024 – Feb 2025 (mostly) | ~25.7 (bills heavily offset by credits) |\n| Oct 2025 – Jan 2026 | 69–90 (smaller credits) |\n| Feb – Jun 2026 | 100 (no credits) |\n| Jul – Aug 2026 | 102.4 |\n\n- Bills in the latest months are roughly **4 times** what the index showed during the periods of heaviest credits in late 2024 and early 2025.\n- Compared with the October 2025 to January 2026 bills, they're roughly **13–48% higher**.\n- In the data, the credits stop after January 2026. That fits the state and federal bill relief schemes winding down. Since February 2026 Perth households have been paying the full tariff, which went up another 2.4% in July.\n\n**Why it feels huge**\nIf your last few bills were reduced by credits and the newer ones aren't, the bill could look several times bigger even though the per-unit price only rose about 2.4%. It's worth checking your older bills for a credit line to confirm.\n\nUsage matters too. Air-conditioning in a hot spell or heating in winter can push a bill up a lot, and CPI doesn't measure that. If your usage and credits haven't changed and the bill still jumped well beyond about 2–3%, look at whether your tariff or plan changed.\n\n**Summary:** Perth power prices have risen far less than in other capitals, but the government credits that used to soften bills have ended. Lots of Perth households will be seeing the same jump.",
